Settings control how NAHPU works on this device: catalog formats, controlled
vocabularies, custom fields, presets, and app preferences.

Open `Settings` from the home screen menu or from a project's `Dashboard` menu.
To configure project-scoped custom fields or a project's `Field ID` values,
open that project first. To walk through initial device setup step by step, use
`Setup NAHPU` from the home screen menu.

## What each setting affects

| Kind | Examples | Transfer behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Project information | Name, accession, location, time zone, dates, project `Field ID` values | Included in project-info and project transfers |
| Shared database records | Taxa and personnel | Stored in the NAHPU database and associated with projects |
| User configs | Controlled vocabularies, field visibility, custom-field definitions, and export or document presets | Transferred with `Export user configs` and `Import user configs` |
| Installation state | Theme, language, regional format, dashboard tools, and active catalog format | Stays on this device |

Controlled vocabularies, presets, and installation preferences are shared by
every project on this device. Changing them while one project is open also
affects other projects on the same installation.

## Catalogs {% #catalogs %}

Across `Sites`, `Events`, and `Specimens`, settings work in two consistent ways:

- **Controlled vocabularies** let you add or remove choices, set a text case
  format (`A`), or pull existing values from your records with `Match database`.
- **Field-visibility toggles** only show or hide built-in fields on forms;
  hiding a field never deletes values already stored in the database.

### Format {% #format %}

`Format` {% ref id="A" /%} sets the active catalog format for new specimen
forms and measurement fields on this device. A single project can still hold
specimens from more than one taxon group.

When `Mammalogy` is selected, `Measurement records` also offers
`Always show bat fields` so bat-specific fields always appear on mammal
measurement forms instead of being toggled per specimen.

### Sites {% #sites %}

`Sites` {% ref id="B" /%} configures site forms and coordinate maps:

- `Map layers`: choose a base layer or import and arrange custom layers
  (GeoJSON, zipped WGS84 Shapefiles, and PMTiles).
- `Geography fields`: choose which geography fields appear on site forms.
- Controlled vocabularies: manage `Site Types`, `Habitat Types`, and `Datums`.

### Events {% #events %}

`Events` {% ref id="C" /%} configures collection event forms:

- `Primary Activities` {% ref id="G" /%}: defaults to `Collecting`,
  `Recording`, and `Observing` (`Other` is not included unless you add it).
- `Collection Methods` and `Personnel Roles`: controlled vocabularies for
  methods and event roles.
- `Environmental data fields`: choose which temperature, humidity, measurement,
  aquatic, astronomy, and note fields appear on event forms. Forms start with
  ambient temperature, ambient humidity, cloud cover, rainfall, and notes.

Transfer user configs to keep these vocabularies identical across field
devices.

### Specimens {% #specimens %}

`Specimens` {% ref id="D" /%} configures specimen capture, numbering,
controlled vocabularies, and symbionts:

- `Capture records`: turn `Always show collector field` on or off.
- Numbering: configure `Field ID`, `Tissue ID`, and (when `Ichthyology` is
  active) `Individual tag ID`, described below.
- `Sex`: restricted controlled vocabulary. `Male`, `Female`, and `Unknown` are
  always available. Use the `+` chips to add `Gynandromorph`, `Hermaphrodite`,
  `Female?`, or `Male?` (a question mark records examiner uncertainty). An
  added option can be removed only when no specimen in the database uses it.
- Controlled vocabularies: manage `Identification Methods`, `Life Stages`,
  `Specimen Types`, `Treatments`, and `Conditions`.
- `Symbionts` (for `Mammalogy`, `Ornithology`, `Herpetology`, and
  `Ichthyology`): configure `Identification` (`Symbiont ID prefix` and
  `Symbiont ID number`) and editable default vocabularies for
  `Interaction types`, `Categories`, `Detection Methods`,
  `Preparation Methods`, `Anatomical Locations`, `Storage`, and `Treatments`.
  See [Symbionts](/en/usages/specimens/symbionts/).

#### Field ID {% #field-id %}

Under `Field ID`, choose how new specimens are numbered:

| Choice | Built from | Requires |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Personnel ID` (default) | The Cataloger's initials and running number | A person with the `Cataloger` role in the project, with initials and a current field number |
| `Project ID` | `Prefix` + `Current catalog number` + `Suffix` | An open project, the three values, and whether `Auto-increment catalog number` is on |

With `Project ID`, NAHPU joins the three values directly: `NAHPU-`, `120`, and
`-M` produce `NAHPU-120-M`. Select `Save field ID settings` to apply changes.

{% aside type="caution" %}

Changing a `Project ID` prefix or suffix changes how already-assigned project
IDs display, and NAHPU asks you to confirm. Check first whether those IDs have
already been exported or written on specimen labels.

{% /aside %}

#### Tissue ID {% #tissue-id %}

`Tissue ID` takes a prefix, a next number, and a suffix, and shows a live
preview of the next tissue ID. Leading zeros are preserved, so `M0042-T` is
followed by `M0043-T`.

When the active catalog format is `Ichthyology`, `Individual tag ID` numbers
individuals in a fish lot the same way. See
[Fishes](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/fishes/#tag-id-numbering).

### Custom fields {% #custom-fields %}

`Custom fields` {% ref id="E" /%} adds your own fields to site, environmental,
specimen, part, and symbiont forms.

| Setting | Options |
| --- | --- |
| Type | `Text`, `Number`, `Yes / No`, or `Dropdown` |
| Scope | `Current project` (this project only) or `Global` (every project on this device), chosen at creation |
| Catalog | Specimen-related fields can be limited to selected catalog formats |
| Darwin Core mapping | Optional export term for the field |

{% steps %}

1. Open the project if the field is project-scoped, go to `Settings` >
   `Custom fields`, choose where the field appears, and select
   `Add custom field`.
2. Enter a `Label` and choose a `Type`. For a dropdown, add the allowed
   options. For a number field, include the unit in the label (such as
   `Canopy height (m)`).
3. Choose the `Scope` and, for specimen-related fields, the applicable catalog
   formats.
4. Optionally set a Darwin Core mapping. NAHPU does not validate values against
   the mapped term, so inspect a sample export before sharing data.
5. Save, enter a test value on a record in that section, and reopen the record
   to confirm it saves properly.
6. To share definitions with another device, export them from `Custom fields`
   (as a file or QR code) or with user configs, then import and verify the
   section, scope, and options on the target device.

{% /steps %}

Use the actions menu on an existing field to reorder it (`Move up` /
`Move down`), archive it, or delete it:

- `Archive` hides a field while keeping its stored data; `Restore` brings it
  back.
- `Delete` appears as `Delete (values in use)` and stays unavailable while any
  record holds a value.
- `Discard legacy values` clears legacy values separately and cannot be undone.

## Database {% #database %}

- `Taxa` {% ref id="F" /%} manages taxonomy records available to projects.
- `Personnel` manages people in the database and their specimen-care roles.
- `Backup database` saves a full ZIP or TAR.GZ archive of all projects,
  records, managed media, and settings.
- `Replace database` restores a NAHPU backup archive (ZIP or TAR.GZ) or SQLite
  file (`.sqlite3`). NAHPU shows a side-by-side comparison before overwriting
  and leaves `Back up current data first` turned on by default.
- `Check database` scans every project for problems that can stop exports or
  hide records, and repairs the safe ones. See
  [Check database](#check-database).

{% aside type="caution" %}

Keep `Back up current data first` turned on, or save a separate backup before
replacing the database. Replacing the database overwrites the whole
installation and is different from merging a project or importing user configs.
See [Restoring a database](../export/#restoring-a-database).

{% /aside %}

### Check database {% #check-database %}

`Check database` scans the whole database, every project included, when the
page opens. Errors stop project exports and data packages; warnings do not.

| Problem | What it means | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Damaged database file | SQLite found damage in the database file itself | Restore a backup with `Replace database` |
| Broken links | A record points to a record that no longer exists | `Clear the link`, or `Delete this record` when the record only belonged to the missing record |
| Links to other projects | A specimen or event points to an event or site in another project | `Clear the link` |
| Missing media files | A media record has no file on this device | `Delete the media record` |
| Missing personnel photos | A personnel photo is no longer on this device | `Remove the photo` |
| Missing measurement records | A specimen has no measurement record for its taxon group | `Add an empty record` |
| Field number conflicts | A specimen has both a personnel field number and a project field number | Fix by hand |
| Duplicate field numbers | Several specimens share a field number | Fix by hand |

The open button beside each problem shows the affected records in the data
table, across all projects. You can select fixes one at a time or with
`Select all fixes`, then select `Fix N selected`. The
`Back up the database first` option is turned on by default and saves a copy
of the database to the NAHPU backup folder, where `Replace database` can
restore the copy. Media files are not part of the copy because no fix deletes
files.

A record that lost its project has no automatic fix, because clearing the
project would hide the record from every project. NAHPU refuses a fix, and
changes nothing, when other records still depend on the record that the fix
would change.

When an export fails, the failure message offers a `Check database` button.

## Exports {% #exports %}

- `Tabular Data` manages reusable presets for exporting tabular records.
- `Documents` manages print-layout presets, document templates, and fonts
  available to templates.
- `Template Editor` creates and edits document templates.

Choose `Load defaults` from the options menu—or from an empty preset list—to
add bundled presets. Presets that work with any catalog format start checked;
catalog-specific presets start unchecked and are also offered in `Setup NAHPU`.
Loading defaults never overwrites an existing preset with the same name, and
any preset can be deleted.

Export these presets as user configs when another installation needs the same
columns, labels, or layouts.

## Applications {% #applications %}

These preferences apply only to this installation.

| Setting | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `Theme` | Switches between `Light`, `Dark`, and `System`. |
| `Language` | Sets the app language (`System default` or a specific language). In beta. |
| `Regional format` | Controls how dates and numbers display on screen (`Match language` or a region). Saved records and exports keep standard formats. In beta. |
| `Tools` | Chooses which tools appear in the project dashboard dock. Use `Reset` to restore defaults. |
| `Data usage` | Reports storage usage and cleans up unlinked files, described below. |

### Data usage and cleanup {% #data-usage-and-cleanup %}

Open `Data usage` to scan NAHPU-managed folders. The summary shows
`Total usage`, `Files`, `Media files`, and `Reclaimable` space from unlinked
files.

- **`Remove unlinked files`** deletes files no longer used by any record, such
  as leftovers from deleted media or cancelled imports. Linked files, databases
  and backups, custom fonts, map layers, and files outside NAHPU-managed
  folders are never affected. Remove installed fonts from `Documents` > `Fonts`
  instead.
- **The `Files` tree** lets you expand or collapse folders (`Expand all` /
  `Collapse all`), inspect stored files, save a copy, remove empty folders, or
  remove eligible files one by one.
- **Multi-file cleanup**: choose `Select`, then pick individual files, folders,
  or `Select all`. Use the bottom toolbar (`Clear`, `Review`, `Done`) to check
  the count, size, and locations before exporting the selection to an archive
  (with an option to remove the originals afterward) or deleting the files
  permanently.
- **`Rescan`** refreshes the report if files changed outside NAHPU.

## Transferring user configs {% #transferring-user-configs %}

Both `Export user configs` and `Import user configs` are available from the
home screen menu, the project `Dashboard` menu, and `Setup NAHPU`—not inside
the `Settings` page.

A user-config transfer lets you pick which sections to share (controlled
vocabularies and field visibility, record-export presets, document templates
and layouts, and custom fields) without replacing the project database. Review
imported configs before replacing your current setup, and see
[Export user configs](../export/#export-user-configs) for full steps.
